Track 1: A Ferrite Core Swallows the Gale
- Story & Context: In the drenched autumn of 1991, pirate technician Arthur Haze watched floodwaters lap against the floorboards of the Cape Lookout transmitter shack while outside a Category 3 squall tore the coastal grid to ribbons. With the backup generator choking on salt spray, he jammed a live zinc grounding cable straight into the antenna chassis of an obsolete magnetic-memory transceiver, sending a sweeping, twelve-minute orchestral progressive loop bleeding across three hundred miles of black ocean just before the circuits melted into slag. His crackling voice barely cut through the surging cello crescendo as the final transmission logged on open tape: "Drive the copper deep before the cloud-wall breaks."
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Cinematic macro view of an antique magnetic ferrite core memory matrix submerged in dark seawater, illuminated from behind by a violent electrical flash, fine copper wires glinting with ocean salt, melancholic album art, fine film grain.

Track 2: Did the Cathode Tell You to Stay?
- Story & Context: Corin spent three winters squatting in the rusted radar dish atop Hatteras Island, obsessively nursing a green-screen cathode monitor that lit her hollow cheeks as hail battered the corrugated sheet-metal roof. She confessed into an open ribbon mic that she had spent thirty months spoofing NOAA storm frequencies with layered orchestral horn swells and rolling house kicks, hoping the broadcast would guide her estranged sister’s adrift scallop boat safely through Diamond Shoals. As the track descends from an unbearable 64-bar riser into a sodden, weeping viola passage, her voice breaks through the radio band: "I painted thunder on the dials so you would turn around."
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: A solitary vintage green monochrome cathode ray screen glowing in a pitch-black, rain-streaked radio room, displaying a spinning weather radar spiral, reflected in a pool of dark water on a rusted steel table, evocative and moody.

Track 3: Elora Vane Beside the Demagnetizer
- Story & Context: Long after the legendary pirate shack collapsed into the tide, beachcombers near Ocracoke still find spools of quarter-inch tape tangled like glistening brown kelp in the spartina grass. Legend holds that Elora Vane, the reclusive classical composer whose symphonic scores fueled the station's final broadcasts, vanished into the salt marshes carrying only an industrial bulk tape eraser and an acoustic violin fitted with a copper pick-up. Local fishermen still swear that whenever barometric pressure drops below 990 millibars, an unpowered car radio left idling on the salt flats will pick up her mournful strings soaring over an unstoppable progressive kick: "The storm remembers what the tape forgot."
